    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e320282a-d753-5114-aed7-fb576099a4d8",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n7px=2x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=7p,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n7p=2.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
